  "extra_brief": "## Germany run notes (October 2026)\n\nThe two official register files are already downloaded. Read them with pandas instead of fetching them again:\n\n- `/Users/baird/Dropbox/_git_ALL/_github-repos-gem/gogpt-researcher/work/sweep_germany/Kraftwerksliste_2026-06-26.xlsx`: the Bundesnetzagentur power plant list, data date 26 June 2026. Sheet `Gesamtkraftwerksliste`, header on row 8 (`pd.read_excel(path, sheet_name=0, header=7)`). Gas rows have `Energietraeger` equal to `Erdgas`. Match a GEM plant by `Anlagenbetreiber` (operator), `Bundesland` and capacity, not by name; GEM plant names are English and the register's `Anzeigename` is usually different. Status is in `Kraftwerksstatus` (exact strings and their GEM mapping are in the country note), start year in `Jahr_Inbetriebnahme`, closure year in `Jahr_Stilllegung**`, nameplate in `Bruttoleistung_MW`, net in `Nettonennleistung_MW`, technology in `Technologie_Stromerzeugung`.\n- `/Users/baird/Dropbox/_git_ALL/_github-repos-gem/gogpt-researcher/work/sweep_germany/ZuUndRueckbau_2026-06-26.xlsx`: expected additions and closures, same data date. Sheet `Zu- und Rückbau einzeln`, header on row 6 (`header=5`): register number, operator, plant name, postcode, town, fuel, net MW, expected year of commercial operation. A unit listed here as expected in 2026 or 2027 is under construction or in trial operation according to the operator's legal report; that supports construction status and a start year, but confirm commissioning itself with the operator or press.\n\nWhen you cite one of these files, use the download URL from the country note as the reference and say in the note which file, which data date and which row (operator and `Anzeigename`) you read. Run `url_verifier.py` on the download URL with the value, as for any other source.\n\nThe capacity tenders under the StromVKG law: the first round closed on 8 September 2026 and awards are due by 3 November 2026, so no award list exists yet. An owner's announcement that it bid, or that it plans to bid, is a Latest Activity entry with the press release as the source, not a status change. If you find an award list published after this brief was written, treat an award as a strong signal but keep the lifecycle rules: no permit and no construction means the status stays announced or pre-construction.\n\nGerman-language sources are expected. Write every note in English. A short German quotation is fine when followed by its English meaning. Permits are usually BImSchG approvals published by the state environment office or the Regierungspräsidium; the town's or district's official notices (Amtsblatt) carry the dates.\n"
}
